From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 08:00:36 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] venus: bound a host image copy by its footprint

vkCopyImageToMemoryMESA points the driver straight at the reply blob so the
temporary allocation and copy are skipped, and vkCopyMemoryToImageMESA
points it at the command data the guest sent.  Both are as long as the guest
chose, while the driver reaches as far as the copy region's footprint, so a
guest that sizes either too small lets the driver write or read past
renderer memory.

Compute that footprint as Vulkan addresses memory for buffer-image copies:
from the region, the image's format and array layer count, which the
renderer now records when the image is created, and the texel block of that
format and aspect as the Vulkan registry's format table gives it.  A format
or aspect the table cannot state exactly is sized at 32 bytes a texel, more
than any format holds, and a MEMCPY copy also counts the driver's memcpy
size for its layers.  A region whose footprint fits the guest's memory is
copied in place.  Otherwise the driver gets renderer memory of the whole
footprint, zeroed for an upload, and only the bytes the guest allocated or
sent move between it and the guest.  A region whose footprint cannot be
stated, because its layers lie outside the image or its size overflows, is
refused.
